 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method uses a 2D grid for computational efficiency, though it can be extended to a 3D grid [S1]. The global generator uses StyleGAN2, which is computationally intensive but allows for efficient convolutional generation of the latent grid [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** The 2D grid representation scales better to large, densely populated scenes than 1D global latent codes used in GRAF and $\pi$-GAN [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Allows extrapolation beyond given observations due to the learned scene prior; handles unconstrained scenes by decomposing them into local regions [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires stochastic weighted sampling to handle invalid camera locations; the 2D grid assumption may not capture vertical variations as well as a 3D grid [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When generating large, unconstrained scenes with diverse viewpoints.
-*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity vertical detail is critical and a 3D grid is necessary, or when computational resources for StyleGAN2 are insufficient.
+*   **Complexity:** The method uses a 2D grid for computational efficiency, though it can be extended to 3D [S1]. The memory usage is determined by the size of the grid $W$ ($c \times s \times s$) and the radiance field network parameters.
+*   **Strengths:** It scales effectively to large, densely populated scenes where single-network radiance fields show diminishing returns [S1]. It allows for explicit control over viewpoint and field of view via camera parameters [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The 2D grid assumption may not capture vertical variations as well as a 3D grid, although the paper notes the framework can be extended [S1]. Sampling invalid camera poses (e.g., inside walls) requires a specific stochastic weighted sampling strategy to mitigate [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Use GSN when generating unconstrained, large-scale scenes with diverse viewpoints, particularly when single-network approaches fail to scale [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** Avoid if the scene is small and simple, where a single radiance field (like NeRF or GRAF) would be more efficient and sufficient [S1].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** GSN was introduced in "Unconstrained Scene Generation with Locally Conditioned Radiance Fields" [S1].
-*   **History and Development:** It builds on the idea of distributing scenes among several smaller networks to avoid the diminishing returns of increasing a single radiance field network's capacity [S1]. It contrasts with dictionary-based memory models that limit unseen viewpoints [S1].
-*   **Usage:** It uses StyleGAN2 architecture for the global generator and discriminator, and CIPS-style modulation for the radiance field network [S1].
+GSN was introduced in the paper "Unconstrained Scene Generation with Locally Conditioned Radiance Fields" [S1]. It builds upon the concept of locally conditioned radiance fields [34] and StyleGAN2 [25]. The method addresses the limitations of 1D global latent codes used in models like GRAF [49] and $\pi$-GAN [3], which do not scale well to large scenes [S1]. It also contrasts with dictionary-based memory models that rely on stored observations and cannot extrapolate to unseen viewpoints [S1].
 
